※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ `⌜x⌝` lifts what is written between the brackets out of the program and into
※ the compiler: `⌜u32⌝` is the type and not a value of it, and `⌜a⌝` is the
※ name and not what it stands for.
※
※ The brackets are what keeps the grammar context-free, which is the whole
※ reason they are there.  A type's name and a value's name are both
※ identifiers, and a type written out in full -- `u8⟦3⟧`, `〈u8, u16〉`, a name
※ the program defined -- is not an expression at all.  Without something saying
※ "what follows is lifted", the parser would have to know what the names turned
※ out to mean before it could read them, which is not a thing a parser can do.
※
※ Two things read a lift.  `⎕typeof` answers the type of what was lifted, and
※ the answer is compared with another lift in a question the compiler settles.
※ And `⌈` and `⌊` written before a lifted type answer the largest and the
※ smallest value that type has -- the same operators and the same word they
※ have everywhere else, asked of a type rather than of something holding
※ several values.

enum Colour:
    red ; green ; blue

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    let a: u32 = 5u32
    let c: Colour = Colour.green
    let v: u8⟦3⟧ = ⟦1u8, 2u8, 3u8⟧ in ⎕static
    let t: 〈u8, u16〉 = 〈1u8, 2u16〉
    ※ the ends of a type, which is what a program otherwise writes out by hand
    let most: u8 = ⌈⌜u8⌝
    let least: i8 = ⌊⌜i8⌝
    ※ including of a width that is not a machine width
    let small: u3 = ⌈⌜u3⌝
    let low: i5 = ⌊⌜i5⌝
    ※ a type the program defined, named in a lift
    comptime if ⎕typeof(⌜c⌝) = ⌜Colour⌝:
        ※ a type written out in full, which is no expression at all
        comptime if ⎕typeof(⌜v⌝) = ⌜u8⟦3⟧⌝:
            comptime if ⎕typeof(⌜t⌝) = ⌜〈u8, u16〉⌝:
                ※ and one that is not the type, asked the other way round
                comptime if ⎕typeof(⌜a⌝) ≠ ⌜u8⌝:
                    if most ≠ 255u8 ∨ least ≠ ⁻128i8:
                        1u6
                    elif small ≠ 7u3 ∨ low ≠ ⁻16i5:
                        2u6
                    ※ a floating-point type's smallest is the most negative
                    ※ number it holds and not the smallest positive one
                    elif ⌈⌜f32⌝ < 1.0f32 ∨ ⌊⌜f32⌝ > ⁻1.0f32:
                        3u6
                    ※ and a code point's ends are the ends Unicode gave it
                    elif ⎕ord(⌈⌜char⌝) ≠ 1114111u32 ∨ ⎕ord(⌊⌜char⌝) ≠ 0u32:
                        4u6
                    else:
                        0u6
                else:
                    5u6
            else:
                6u6
        else:
            7u6
    else:
        8u6
